# The Supabase structure backup is verified byte-for-byte.
#
# `tests/test_schema_backup.py` hashes the file on disk and compares it to
# `supabase/backups/SHA256SUMS`, which is computed over LF content. Git's
# `core.autocrlf` is on by default on Windows, so a checkout there rewrites every
# line ending and the hash no longer matches — the committed blob is correct, the
# working tree is not, and the two differ by nothing but bytes git inserted.
#
# The failure reads as "the recovery artifact is corrupt", on the one file whose
# whole purpose is restoring the live database structure. That is the worst place
# to have a false alarm, and the reason it is pinned rather than the test loosened:
# a checksum that tolerates a rewrite cannot detect the corruption it exists for.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions, so the file is
# checked out exactly as committed on every platform.
supabase/backups/*.sql -text
supabase/backups/SHA256SUMS -text
